There is a beautifully simple invariant

For every proposed business, ask:

$$ \boxed{ \text{Can OpenAI destroy this merely by writing more software?} } $$

If yes, dangerous.

If no, ask why.

The interesting answers are:

because I own the data-generation process
because I have permission
because I own the physical thing
because I have the customers
because I have marketplace liquidity
because I'm licensed
because people trust me
because I have proprietary outcomes
because I have a lower physical cost structure
because I have exclusive access

Those are the moats.

This also changes our interpretation of MCP completely

The Official MCP Registry is already full of thousands of capabilities. It includes things like live building permits, company technographics, ad exchanges, scholarly databases, real-estate transaction records and tools acting on real websites.

The interesting question isn't:

Which one can I turn into a plugin?

That's temporary arbitrage.

The interesting question is:

Which MCP represents an actual scarce underlying economic resource?

That's what I'd mine.

For each MCP:

MCP
 ↓
What is behind this endpoint?
 ↓
┌─────────────────────────────┐
│ OSS computation only?       │ → ZERO MOAT
│ public data transformed?    │ → LOW
│ proprietary live data?      │ → HIGH
│ authenticated actions?      │ → HIGH
│ marketplace/network?        │ → VERY HIGH
│ physical execution?         │ → VERY HIGH
│ regulated authority?        │ → VERY HIGH
└─────────────────────────────┘

Now that is a worthwhile MCP miner.

And this gives you a substantially better Seesaw system

Instead of:

find plugin opportunities.

Make it:

find newly valuable external scarcities as cognition becomes free.

Formally:

$$ M_i = E_i \times X_i \times F_i \times N_i \times T_i \times \beta_i $$

where:

\(E\) = exclusivity/access;
\(X\) = externality — can't be synthesized by reasoning;
\(F\) = feedback/trajectory compounding;
\(N\) = network effects;
\(T\) = trust/authority;
\(\beta\) = positive sensitivity to better AI.

Then subtract:

$$ PublicReplicability + PlatformCapture + SyntheticSubstitutability. $$

This is much stronger than our Plugin Opportunity Score.

Your projects look different through this lens

Some of the stuff you've been building suddenly makes more sense than “plugin factory.”

Cmail / voiceagent: valuable if it controls real identities/numbers/domains and gives agents permission to communicate. Wrapper alone is worthless.

Company graph: valuable if it accumulates difficult, changing, verified company state. Public-data aggregation alone is weaker.

Breadup/Etsy commerce: the UI isn't the moat. Transactions, seller histories, pricing outcomes and actual marketplace behavior could become proprietary trajectory data.

Security: strong because the environment is adversarial and constantly produces new state; better AI produces both stronger attack and defense demand.

XMR/agent commerce: interesting if it gives agents actual settlement/escrow/private commerce capability, rather than another wallet UI.

GeoDrop: potentially interesting precisely where it establishes difficult-to-reconstruct installed-base/physical-world information.

Those survive the “OpenAI writes the software tomorrow” test much better.

So I would make one major strategic change

Do not build the plugin compiler.

Assume OpenAI releases it.

Don't even build routing optimization as the long-term company. The tool-retrieval work suggests that will also get absorbed.

Instead, use plugins/MCP as the distribution protocol for scarce assets you own.

The game becomes:

$$ \boxed{ \text{Acquire/produce scarce capability} \rightarrow \text{expose it through whatever agent protocol wins} } $$

not:

$$ \text{be really good at the protocol}. $$

The protocol should disappear.

And OpenAI's commerce trajectory is already a concrete example: ACP is becoming standardized and increasingly easy to integrate, while the actual value remains with merchants who own products, inventory, fulfillment and customer relationships. OpenAI explicitly designed ACP so the merchant remains merchant-of-record and controls fulfillment/support.

That may be the general pattern of the agent economy:

interfaces become free; ownership of consequential state does not.
	